Z||000028||Ryan Wright||08-13-2001||07:02 PM||||Hey Mike,<P>The DeLorean almost IS a Fiero, and is the only car I could see myself owning in place of a Fiero. The body panels are all fiberglass; the stainless steel is simply overlaid on top of the glass. It's got a rear mounted 2.8 V6. You don't get a trunk - they move it forward and you have a little shelf behind the seats instead. And, obviously, it looks just like a Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Performance is right on par with a V6 Fiero as well...<P>You can buy a low milage DeLorean in great condition for $17k-$20k. Even the beaters still go for close to $10k. DeLoreans are often misunderstood, just like Fieros. Despite what many believe, they don't break down any more often than the next car. They're really a great vehicle. <P>Before someone mentions it, no, John DeLorean was not a crack dealer. He was setup by the US Government and was cleared of all charges under laws regulating entrapment. DeLorean Motor Company was already in financial jeopardy and John DeLorean's arrest was one of the final nails in the coffin. Mr. DeLorean is also a very nice guy - he's made appearances at DeLorean shows, has signed people's dashboards for them, and even allowed other DeLorean owners (for the most part complete strangers) into his home just to "hang out" & chat.<P>------------------<BR>-Ryan Wright (http://www.ryanwright.com)||||reg||1
Z||000029||MikeSchaefer||08-13-2001||10:17 PM||||Hmm, I just got rid of my Delorean (29,000 mile) and I'll have to say that it does break down a lot more than our Fieros do.  They really aren't any nicer than a Fiero, the PRV-60 is noticably slower than our 2.8 and the handling is almost identical, maybe a little sloppier.  The only thing better about the Delorean is that it is kind of a celebrity to drive around, everyone loves it.  There is a lot more storage space in a Delorean, behind the seat and up front, but I never used the space.  The Delorean is about 3 inches longer than a fiero, and about an inch higher off the ground (my estimate from looking at them together in the garage).  I will tell anyone that goes from a Fiero to a Delorean, that they'll be dissapointed, the Fiero is a hundred times easier to work on, buy parts for, and is much better made.  <BR>Later,<BR>Mike Schaefer <A HREF="http://surf.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://surf.to/fiero</A> <BR>you can see pics of my old D on the site above, in the hybrid fiero section.||||reg||1
